From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: a landed knob for the HS400 data-strobe delay

The HS400 strobe sweep scores every candidate delay with mmc_get_ext_csd() --
a READ. The deficit this tree is chasing is in writes. This adds the
instrument that lets one board session produce the write-scored curve, because
the write-scored sweep itself cannot live in the kernel.

THE OBJECTIVE MISMATCH
----------------------
`mmc-sunxi-tune-the-hs400-data-strobe-delay` sweeps SMHC_DS_DL 0..63 and keeps the midpoint of the widest run of
delays for which mmc_get_ext_csd() succeeds. That is a read of 512 bytes framed
by the card's data strobe, and it is the right objective for read data.

In HS400 the card drives the data strobe for more than read data: every written
block is answered by a CRC status token on DAT0, and the controller has a
register for how it detects that token (SMHC_CSDC, the CRC status detect control,
set to the HS400 code by `mmc-sunxi-add-a733-emmc-hs400`). A per-block status that the write path waits on
is exactly where a per-block cost would live, and the measurement says the cost
is per block:

  the 8 GiB board, DV4032, HS400 200 MHz 8 bit, 1 MiB requests, mmc_request_start ->
  mmc_request_done:  ours 7084.6 us, vendor 6240.1 us
  per 512-byte block: 3.459 us against 3.047 us, with the raw bus time at
  200 MHz DDR x8 being 1.28 us.

And the two stacks sit at different points of the delay line:

  SMHC_DS_DL 0x148   ours 0x00000B8B   vendor 0x00000282

decoded with UM 11.3.8.38 (same field layout as SMHC_SAMP_DL, 11.3.8.37):
[5:0] software delay, [7] software enable, [13:8] read-only runtime state. So
ours runs software delay 11 with the enable set, the vendor runs 2 -- and both
read back their own value in [13:8], which is what says the writes landed.

WHY THE FIX IS NOT "SCORE IT WITH A WRITE"
------------------------------------------
Scoring a write means writing to the card. A host driver running inside
->execute_hs400_tuning has no scratch area it may spend: the callback runs
during card init, before any partition table is known, and every sector belongs
to somebody. There is no safe in-kernel write objective, so this patch does not
invent one.

WHAT IT ADDS INSTEAD
--------------------
One sysfs attribute per sunxi-mmc platform device, hs400_ds_dl, RW, -ENODEV on
controllers without the strobe path:

    read:  "<driver value> cfg <cfg default> reg 0x<SMHC_DS_DL as it reads>"
    write: 0..63, sets host->ds_dl and, when the bus is in HS400, the register

so the curve is one shell loop:

    for i in $(seq 0 63); do
        echo $i > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/sunxi-mmc/4022000.mmc/hs400_ds_dl
        <fixed raw-region write benchmark>
    done

Three things make it an instrument rather than a poke:

- The access is taken with pm_runtime_get_sync() and the driver's own spinlock.
  ⚠ mmc_claim_host() is NOT usable here: it is declared in
  drivers/mmc/core/core.h, a core-internal header that host drivers do not
  include, and no in-tree host driver calls it (6.18.36). The
  register block is runtime gated and reads all-zero when idle; the earlier
  attempt at this curve (`mmc-sunxi-a733-hs400-data-strobe-delay-value`'s header) was taken by writing the register
  between transfers and had to be recorded as NOT conclusive precisely because
  some of those writes may never have landed. Reading the register back in the
  same claimed section is what removes that doubt.
- host->ds_dl is set too, not only the register, so the value survives the
  runtime suspend/resume that reprograms the HS400 timing from host state
  (sunxi_mmc_runtime_resume -> sunxi_mmc_set_clk -> sunxi_mmc_hs400_enable).
- The store logs what it set and what the register then read, so the serial
  capture carries the curve's x-axis even if the benchmark's output is lost.

It does not replace the sweep: the sweep still runs at every init and still
picks the read-scored midpoint. This overrides it afterwards, for the life of
the mode.

WHERE THE VALUES COME FROM
--------------------------
Register and field layout: a733-um-v0.92, 11.3.8.38 (SMHC_DS_DL, offset 0x0148,
default 0x0000_2000) and 11.3.8.37 for the identically shaped SMHC_SAMP_DL.
The two register values and the per-block timings are silicon readback on the 8 GiB board
under load, dated 2026-09-19, with the card identified as SanDisk iNAND
SDINBDV4-32G (DV4032, eMMC 5.1). No value comes from any kernel.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
- Applies clean at fuzz 0 on a fresh unpack with the series in order: yes.
- Builds: NOT claimed, not built here.
- Silicon: NOT proven, and this patch's whole purpose is one session that
  produces the curve. The observable that falsifies it as an instrument: write
  a delay, read the attribute back, and the "reg" field must show that delay in
  [5:0] with bit 7 set while the bus is in HS400. If the register does not
  follow the write, the knob is not landing and the curve taken with it would
  be worth nothing -- which is exactly the failure the earlier sweep could not
  rule out.
  The observable that falsifies the hypothesis behind it: a full 0..63 write
  curve on the 8 GiB board whose maximum sits at the same delay the read-scored sweep
  picks. Then the objective mismatch is not real and the read-scored sweep can
  stay as it is.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -3027,12 +3027,131 @@
 	RUNTIME_PM_OPS(sunxi_mmc_runtime_suspend, sunxi_mmc_runtime_resume, NULL)
 };
 
+/*
+ * The HS400 data-strobe delay, as a knob -- the instrument for the one
+ * measurement the in-kernel sweep cannot take.
+ *
+ * sunxi_mmc_execute_hs400_tuning() scores each of the 64 delays with
+ * mmc_get_ext_csd(), which is a READ. That is the right objective for framing
+ * read data and the wrong one for the write path, and the write path is where
+ * this board loses: 139.1 MiB/s against the vendor stack's 152.5 on the same
+ * card, with the whole difference inside mmc_request_start ->
+ * mmc_request_done. In HS400 the card drives the strobe for its CRC status
+ * token as well as for read data, so the delay that best centres a read is not
+ * necessarily the delay that best centres the per-block status the write path
+ * waits on -- and the sweep lands on 11 where the vendor runs 2.
+ *
+ * A write-scored sweep cannot live in this callback: scoring a write means
+ * writing to the card, and there is no scratch area a host driver may spend.
+ * What can live here is the knob that lets a session produce the curve:
+ *
+ *     for i in $(seq 0 63); do
+ *         echo $i > /sys/bus/platform/drivers/sunxi-mmc/<dev>/hs400_ds_dl
+ *         <raw-region write benchmark, fixed size, fixed offset>
+ *     done
+ *
+ * Writing it sets host->ds_dl as well as the register, so the value survives
+ * the runtime suspend/resume that reprograms the HS400 timing from host state;
+ * reading it back reports the driver's value, the cfg default and the register
+ * as it actually reads, because the previous attempt at this curve was taken by
+ * poking the register between transfers and could not tell a write that landed
+ * from one that did not (`mmc-sunxi-a733-hs400-data-strobe-delay-value`'s header says so). The register access is taken
+ * with the controller resumed and the host claimed, which is what makes that
+ * difference.
+ *
+ * The knob does not replace the sweep. The sweep still runs and still picks the
+ * read-scored midpoint at every init; this overrides it afterwards, for as long
+ * as the mode lasts.
+ */
+static ssize_t hs400_ds_dl_show(struct device *dev,
+				struct device_attribute *attr, char *buf)
+{
+	struct mmc_host *mmc = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	struct sunxi_mmc_host *host = mmc_priv(mmc);
+	unsigned long flags;
+	u32 reg;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!host->cfg->can_hs400)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	ret = pm_runtime_get_sync(host->dev);
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(host->dev);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&host->lock, flags);
+	reg = mmc_readl(host, REG_DS_DL_REG);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&host->lock, flags);
+
+	pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(host->dev);
+
+	return sysfs_emit(buf, "%u cfg %u reg 0x%08x\n",
+			  host->ds_dl, host->cfg->ds_dl, reg);
+}
+
+static ssize_t hs400_ds_dl_store(struct device *dev,
+				 struct device_attribute *attr,
+				 const char *buf, size_t count)
+{
+	struct mmc_host *mmc = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	struct sunxi_mmc_host *host = mmc_priv(mmc);
+	u32 val, reg;
+	unsigned long flags;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!host->cfg->can_hs400)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	ret = kstrtou32(buf, 0, &val);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	if (val > SDXC_DS_DL_SW_MASK)
+		return -ERANGE;
+
+	ret = pm_runtime_get_sync(host->dev);
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(host->dev);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&host->lock, flags);
+	host->ds_dl = val;
+	if (mmc->ios.timing == MMC_TIMING_MMC_HS400) {
+		reg = mmc_readl(host, REG_DS_DL_REG);
+		reg &= ~SDXC_DS_DL_SW_MASK;
+		reg |= val | SDXC_CAL_DL_SW_EN;
+		mmc_writel(host, REG_DS_DL_REG, reg);
+		reg = mmc_readl(host, REG_DS_DL_REG);
+	} else {
+		reg = 0;
+	}
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&host->lock, flags);
+
+	pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(host->dev);
+
+	dev_info(mmc_dev(mmc),
+		 "HS400: data-strobe delay set to %u by sysfs, register reads 0x%08x\n",
+		 val, reg);
+
+	return count;
+}
+static DEVICE_ATTR_RW(hs400_ds_dl);
+
+static struct attribute *sunxi_mmc_attrs[] = {
+	&dev_attr_hs400_ds_dl.attr,
+	NULL,
+};
+ATTRIBUTE_GROUPS(sunxi_mmc);
+
 static struct platform_driver sunxi_mmc_driver = {
 	.driver = {
 		.name	= "sunxi-mmc",
 		.probe_type = PROBE_PREFER_ASYNCHRONOUS,
 		.of_match_table = sunxi_mmc_of_match,
 		.pm = pm_ptr(&sunxi_mmc_pm_ops),
+		.dev_groups = sunxi_mmc_groups,
 	},
 	.probe		= sunxi_mmc_probe,
 	.remove		= sunxi_mmc_remove,
